<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Crisis</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oka crisis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1990">1990</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Kanesatake resistance</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Oka crisis was a 78-day armed stand-off in 1990 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawk</a> protesters and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a> police and Canadian army near the town of Oka, west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a>. It began when Mohawks of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanesatake">Kanesatake</a> blocked a road to stop a golf course from being extended onto disputed land, including a burial ground, and a police officer was killed in a raid. It drew national attention to indigenous land claims in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oka-crisis.jpg" alt="Oka crisis" width="320" height="146" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SamuelFreli · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Crise_d%27Oka_-_SQ_et_forces_canadiennes.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Kanien'kehá:ka, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawk</a> nation of the Haudenosaunee, had moved north from the Hudson valley to the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-lawrence-river">St. Lawrence</a>, forming the communities of Kahnawá:ke, Kanehsatà:ke and Ahkwesáhsne. After the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-france">New France</a> in 1760, the Act of Capitulation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a> guaranteed that the peoples allied to the French could stay on their lands unless these were ceded to the Crown, as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-proclamation-of-1763">Royal Proclamation of 1763</a> restated. Claims at Kahnawá:ke and Ahkwesáhsne were recognised, but Kanehsatà:ke's were ignored, and the Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> extended official title over the disputed land to the Sulpicians eight years after Lord Elgin had refused to recognise their claims in 1851.</p><p>In 1990, after a court found for the developers, the mayor of Oka, Jean Ouellette, announced that the rest of the Pines would be cleared to extend the golf course to eighteen holes and build 60 condominiums. Protesters ignored a court injunction of late April and a second order of 29 June to remove their barricade.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>At 5:15 a.m. on 11 July around a hundred officers of the Sûreté du Québec, called in by the mayor, surrounded the barricade. After the gun battle the number of armed Kanien'kehá:ka grew from about 30 to 60–70 and later to 600; they seized vehicles and built a new barricade across Route 344. The police then sealed off Kahnawá:ke and Kanehsatà:ke, stopping food, medicine and ambulances, while nightly crowds gathered at the blockaded Mercier Bridge.</p><p>On 27 August Premier Robert Bourassa requisitioned the Canadian Armed Forces, and Prime Minister Brian Mulroney named Chief Justice Alan B. Gold as mediator. When families tried to leave Kahnawá:ke, a crowd stoned their cars; the elder Joe Armstrong, 71, died of a heart attack the next day that doctors attributed to stress. Soldiers then replaced the police around both communities.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>After 26 days of siege without supplies, the land defenders walked out on 26 September and were arrested; during the arrests a soldier stabbed 14-year-old Waneek Horn-Miller with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayonet">bayonet</a> near the heart. The crisis was the first widely publicised violent conflict between First Nations and provincial governments in the late 20th century, and it is the subject of Alanis Obomsawin's documentary Kanehsatake: 270 Years of Resistance (1993) and Tracey Deer's film Beans (2020).</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Oka Crisis” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Mohawk communities today include Akwesasne, Kahnawake and Kanesatake, scene of the 1990 <b>Oka crisis</b>, along with Six Nations and Tyendinaga in Ontario.</blockquote><a href="/state/mohawk-nation">Mohawk Nation</a></li></ul></section>
